+import Foundation
+import Metal
+import QuartzCore
+import UIKit
+
+/**
+ Marker views drawn inside the map's own render pass, so they move with the map in the same
+ frame instead of trailing it. JS keeps rendering each marker as a marker view (the "ghost"),
+ which stays the look and the touch target; this class snapshots the ghost's layer tree into a
+ bitmap and draws it as a screen-aligned sprite at the marker's coordinate, centred like the
+ marker view's default anchor and snapped to whole pixels so it stays crisp. Once a snapshot is
+ in, a ghost JS marks hidden is hidden on screen by an empty mask on the view annotation that
+ hosts it: a mask leaves hit testing alone, where alpha under 1% would end it, and the ghost's own
+ layer renders unmasked.
+
+ Snapshots are taken on the main thread only when JS reports a change (a new `version`), then
+ every frame for `burstMs` after it, which covers the mount landing a frame late (the ghost is
+ looked up again until it is there), entering animations, pressed feedback and image decodes. The
+ render side uploads a snapshot once and then only draws the texture.
+
+ State is shared with `RNMBXPuck` under its lock; the puck's layer host calls
+ `renderingWillStart`, `render` and `renderingWillEnd`. Draw order is the order JS sends.
+
+ @parity /node_modules/@rnmapbox/maps/android/src/main/java/com/rnmapbox/rnmbx/components/camera/RNMBXMarkerSprites.kt
+ @parity /src/components/map/markerLayerChannel.ts `createMarkerLayerChannel`
+ @platform-diff The ghost hides through a mask on its host annotation, where Android uses the
+ ghost's own alpha: a Fabric hit test skips a view under 1% alpha. Each sprite keeps a small pool
+ of textures and only writes one no command buffer is still reading, since Metal reads textures
+ after the frame's encoding returns, where GL orders the upload itself.
